# `vercel deploy` from a checkout uploads the working tree as it is, and it does not read .gitignore.
# Without this file it walks into the generated native projects — gigabytes, and CocoaPods symlinks
# the upload rejects — and, worse, into local .env files and real bank exports.
#
# An allowlist at the root, so a new top-level file stays on your machine until somebody decides
# the build needs it.
#
# turbo.json is left out on purpose. The Vercel builds (`pnpm --filter web build` for the web project,
# `pnpm build` in apps/site for the site project) use no Turborepo, and with that file in the upload
# the Vercel build breaks. It stays in the repository, where `pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm test` need it.
/*
!/apps
!/packages
!/package.json
!/pnpm-lock.yaml
!/pnpm-workspace.yaml
!/tsconfig.base.json
!/vercel.json
!/.nvmrc

# The docs library of the site (spec 0.8b) renders these files in place at build time.
!/docs
/docs/*
!/docs/self-hosting.md
!/docs/building-android.md
!/docs/building-ios.md
!/docs/diagrams
!/SECURITY.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md

# apps/mobile itself stays: the install runs with --frozen-lockfile, and the lockfile has an importer
# for it. What it generates does not.
/apps/mobile/ios
/apps/mobile/android
/apps/mobile/build
/apps/mobile/.*.cjs
**/.expo
**/.next
**/.astro
**/dist
**/.turbo
**/node_modules
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets and signing material, wherever they turn up.
**/.env*
!**/.env.example
*.keystore
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.pem
*.key
*.mobileprovision
*.ipa
*.apk
*.aab
